---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeBinary from '@/components/tools/text/DevEncodeBinary.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="decimal-to-binary">
  <DevEncodeBinary client:load kind="number" direction="encode" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The method, shown for your number</h2>
    <p>
      Divide by two and write down the remainder. Divide the quotient by two again, and repeat until the
      quotient is zero. The remainders, read from the bottom up, give the binary form. The table above
      does this for your number, row by row, and shows the working as well as the answer.
    </p>
    <pre><code>Convert 13:
13 ÷ 2 = 6  remainder 1
 6 ÷ 2 = 3  remainder 0
 3 ÷ 2 = 1  remainder 1
 1 ÷ 2 = 0  remainder 1
Read upwards: 1101</code></pre>

    <h2>The subtraction method</h2>
    <p>
      Some people find this method quicker. Write the powers of two down to your number, then subtract the
      largest that fits, again and again:
    </p>
    <pre><code>Convert 200, using 128 64 32 16 8 4 2 1:
200 − 128 = 72   →  1 in the 128 column
 72 −  64 =  8   →  1 in the 64 column
  8 −   8 =  0   →  1 in the 8 column
Result: 11001000</code></pre>

    <h2>Numbers worth knowing by sight</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Decimal</th><th>Binary</th><th>Hex</th><th>Where you meet it</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>15</td><td>1111</td><td>F</td><td>One hex digit, four bits</td></tr>
          <tr><td>16</td><td>10000</td><td>10</td><td>Where hex carries</td></tr>
          <tr><td>127</td><td>1111111</td><td>7F</td><td>Largest signed 8-bit value; end of ASCII</td></tr>
          <tr><td>128</td><td>10000000</td><td>80</td><td>The high bit alone</td></tr>
          <tr><td>255</td><td>11111111</td><td>FF</td><td>Largest byte; an IP octet maximum</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1024</td><td>10000000000</td><td>400</td><td>One kibibyte</td></tr>
          <tr><td>65535</td><td>16 ones</td><td>FFFF</td><td>Largest 16-bit value; the highest port number</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Remember the pattern: n ones equal 2ⁿ − 1, and a 1 followed by n zeros equals 2ⁿ. Almost all
      &ldquo;why does it stop there?&rdquo; limits in computing are one of these two.
    </p>

    <h2>Negative numbers and two's complement</h2>
    <p>
      Hardware does not store a minus sign. It stores the two's complement: invert each bit of the positive
      value and add one. Enter a negative number above, and the panel shows the exact bit pattern at 8, 16,
      32 or 64 bits.
    </p>
    <pre><code>−5 in 8 bits:
 5        = 00000101
inverted  = 11111010
add 1     = 11111011   (251 if read as unsigned)</code></pre>
    <p>
      With two's complement, ordinary binary addition works without change for negative numbers. The cost
      is an asymmetric range: an 8-bit signed value goes from −128 to +127, not −127 to +127, and the most
      negative value has no positive counterpart.
    </p>

    <h2>Very large numbers</h2>
    <p>
      All arithmetic here uses BigInt, so a 200-digit input converts exactly. Ordinary JavaScript numbers
      lose precision above 2⁵³ (about 9 quadrillion), so converters that use them return wrong digits for
      large values without a warning. Paste a very large number and check the round-trip on the
      binary-to-decimal page.
    </p>

    <h2>What about fractions?</h2>
    <p>
      This page handles integers. Fractional binary is a different topic: 0.1 in decimal has no finite
      binary form, as 1/3 has no finite decimal form. This fact explains why <code>0.1 + 0.2</code> is not
      <code>0.3</code> in almost all programming languages.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
